be293082" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065112 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064243 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065232 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065233 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065232 #edb/ref 1061 4226 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065232 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c96b380f-2e0a-5243-a862-5219272b92d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065232 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186065341 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065233 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065256 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065233 #edb/ref 1061 4227 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065233 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d98ff5d5-8ca1-5834-941d-13d2afb9da6f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065233 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064325 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065234 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065235 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065234 #edb/ref 1061 4230 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065234 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1253a4a8-20c7-58c3-9e8e-893e406761f2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065234 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064326 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065235 #edb/ref 1061 4232 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065235 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "59f4cd9a-a9f4-5779-b601-96d0642330dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065235 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064327 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065256 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065234 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065256 #edb/ref 1061 6169 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065256 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5e99daa1-dbe7-5fe2-bda6-1e302fb1a276" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065256 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186065345 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065341 #edb/ref 1064 "A **perfect square trinomial** is a three-term expression that is the result of squaring a binomial.\n\nFor example, the expression\n\n$$\nx^2 + 6x + 9\n$$\n\nis a perfect square trinomial because it is the result of squaring the binomial $(x+3)$, as shown below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+6x+9 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo factor a perfect square trinomial, we have to figure out what binomial it is the square of. We can often do this by relating the perfect square trinomial to the **square of a sum**:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}2}  {\\color{blue}a}x + {\\color{blue}a}^2 = (x + {\\color{blue}a})^2\n$$\n\n \n\nTo illustrate, let's factor the expression $x^2+6x+9$ using the square of sum formula. We perform the following steps:\n\n1\\. Write the constant term as a perfect square:\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+6x+{3}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n2\\. Factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n3\\. Factor the trinomial using the \"square of a sum formula\". In this case, we have ${\\color{blue}{a}}={\\color{blue}{3}}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\nThis method works *only* when the trinomial is a perfect square! It will *not* work for every trinomial. We will learn more general methods in the future." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065341 #edb/ref 1065 1596 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065341 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c04b9f03-f29f-5feb-8d13-7de9fc378a34" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065341 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
